WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.792 --> 00:00:04.462
The Okavango Delta a biodiversity hotspot

00:00:04.546 --> 00:00:07.716
in the heart of one of Africa's
most important

00:00:07.716 --> 00:00:09.759
freshwater systems.

00:00:13.763 --> 00:00:14.639
Its pulse

00:00:14.639 --> 00:00:19.519
is maintained by a river structure
that begins deep in the Angolan highlands,

00:00:19.602 --> 00:00:23.189
in an area locals call Lisima Iya Mwono,

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:25.275
the source of life.

00:00:25.483 --> 00:00:27.694
One half is a constant flow.

00:00:27.694 --> 00:00:32.574
The Cuito river is steady, carrying,
nutrient rich water from Angola's

00:00:32.574 --> 00:00:37.287
hidden source lakes, released slowly
through miles of peatlands.

00:00:37.871 --> 00:00:39.998
The other half drives the pulse.

00:00:39.998 --> 00:00:43.668
The Cubango is unpredictable, 
rocky and rough.

00:00:43.668 --> 00:00:47.797
His water flows faster, pumping
seasonal flows into the system.

00:00:48.882 --> 00:00:51.843
On Namibia's border with the Angola,
these two lifelines

00:00:51.843 --> 00:00:53.511
become the Kavango River,

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:00.518
which becomes

00:01:00.518 --> 00:01:04.481
the Okavango River in Botswana,
where reedbeds and floating

00:01:04.481 --> 00:01:09.527
papyrus islands filter the water
before diffusing into the Okavango Delta.

00:01:13.364 --> 00:01:16.493
This system is as vital as it is delicate.

00:01:16.743 --> 00:01:19.913
If anything begins to affect it,
the consequences

00:01:19.913 --> 00:01:21.414
could be catastrophic.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.960
That's why National Geographic and

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:29.631
De Beers are working through the 
Okavango Eternal Partnership.

00:01:30.131 --> 00:01:32.675
Building on the work done
by local communities

00:01:32.675 --> 00:01:36.179
to know the rivers and the ecology
that keeps them alive.

00:01:37.263 --> 00:01:39.599
Through intense expeditions, 

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:44.729
cutting edge research technologies
and expert indigenous guidance,

00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:48.817
they are building an understanding
of how this ecosystem works.

00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:52.237
Sharing their research
with local organizations

00:01:52.487 --> 00:01:55.907
and policymakers to better protect it.

00:01:55.990 --> 00:01:57.742
Keeping a finger on the pulse.

00:01:58.159 --> 00:02:01.704
Making sure the heart of
the Okavango keeps beating.

